<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Martin V</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1369">1369</a>–<a href="/years/1431">1431</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Oddone Colonna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martin V, born Oddone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a>, was pope from 1417 to 1431. His election at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a>, during which rival popes had competed for nearly 40 years. He returned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, restored its authority over the Papal States and resisted the conciliar movement's claims to authority over the pope.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v.jpg" alt="Pope Martin V" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Venetian school / After Pisanello · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papa_Martino_V.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Oddone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a> was born at Genazzano, between 26 January and 20 February in 1368 or 1369, the son of Agapito Colonna and Caterina Conti. He studied law at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-pavia">University of Pavia</a>, became apostolic protonotary under Pope Urban VI, and was created Cardinal-Deacon of San Giorgio in Velabro by Pope Innocent VII in 1405. In 1409 he took part in the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and supported Antipope Alexander V and then John XXIII. He followed John in his flight to Schaffhausen on 21 March 1415, but returned to Constance and took part in the proceedings that deposed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> was long divided by the rival claims of Pope Gregory XII and Antipope Benedict XIII, until Gregory resigned and Benedict was deposed. Oddone was elected on 11 November 1417 by 23 cardinals and 30 delegates of the council, and took the name Martin from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-tours">Martin of Tours</a>, whose feast fell that day. He was ordained priest on 13 November and consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> the next day. The election effectively ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a>.</p><p>Martin left Constance in May 1418 and travelled slowly, lingering at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> only in September 1420. The pope then ruled little more than Rome and its surroundings, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braccio-da-montone">Braccio da Montone</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> and local vicars held much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>. In Rome he restored churches, palaces and bridges, engaged masters of the Tuscan school, and helped start the Roman Renaissance. From 1423 his main concern was war with Braccio, who was defeated at the Battle of L'Aquila on 2 June 1424 and died a few days later.</p><p>In 1420 he issued a bull calling for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the Wycliffites, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> and other heretics, and in 1428 he ordered the remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wycliffe">John Wycliffe</a> dug up and burned. The crusades against the Lollards ultimately failed. He summoned a council in 1423 that met at Pavia and then Siena, was poorly attended, and was dissolved; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a> opened in February 1431. On 9 December 1425 he founded the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a>, at the request of Duke John IV of Brabant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Martin V died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> of a stroke on 20 February 1431, aged 63, and is buried at St. John Lateran. In 1423 he ruled that annuities redeemable at the seller's option were lawful, which allowed them to be widely used in commerce as a way round the ban on interest. He also confirmed the privileges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in bulls of 1418 and 1422, although he later repealed several of his ordinances.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Martin V” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sigismund could take his kingdom only by force, and <b>Pope Martin V</b> declared a crusade.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hussites">Hussites · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The order, confirmed by <b>Pope Martin V</b>, was carried out in 1428, when his corpse, or a neighbour's, was exhumed and burned.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-4">4 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Council of Constance elected <b>Oddone Colonna</b> as Pope Martin V, having deposed or accepted the resignation of the remaining papal claimants.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-11">11 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1414 to 1418 it hosted the Council of Constance, which elected <b>Pope Martin V</b>, ending the Western Schism, and burned Jan Hus.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-constance">Free Imperial City of Constance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Genazzano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.83,12.97&amp;year=1409" aria-label="Genazzano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4866 674 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Martin V"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5316 944h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5316 944h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.83,12.97&amp;year=1409">What was here in 1409? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
